Answer for item 1 - Azure policies needed to be exempted for the initial Datadog + Azure integration

Based on the PoC review, we should not request any broad tenant-wide Azure Policy exemptions.

We split this into two areas:

1. Datadog App Registration / Service Principal / RBAC
2. Datadog Log Forwarder resources

For App Registration / Service Principal / RBAC:
No Azure Policy exemption is expected.

This part should be done via Terraform/IaC:
- create dedicated Datadog Integration App Registration / Service Principal via tf-common application module
- grant baseline Microsoft Graph permissions only:
  - Application.Read.All
  - Directory.Read.All
  - Group.Read.All
  - Policy.Read.All
  - User.Read.All
- grant Monitoring Reader only on agreed subscription / management group scope
- no tenant-wide RBAC assignment by default

Datadog reference for baseline Microsoft Graph permissions:
https://docs.datadoghq.com/integrations/guide/azure-graph-api-permissions/

Microsoft RBAC least privilege reference:
https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/role-based-access-control/best-practices

The PoC App Registration had a wider permission set. We should not copy it to production by default.
Additional permissions like Domain.Read.All, EntitlementManagement.Read.All, PrivilegedAccess* or RoleManagement* should be handled separately and only added if required by a concrete Datadog feature, e.g. Cloud Security / CIEM / Identity Risks.

For Log Forwarder resources:
Policy exemptions may be required, but only for the Datadog Log Forwarder control plane resources.

PoC showed conflicts with the following policies:
- Storage account infrastructure encryption is not enabled
- Function apps should disable public network access
- App Service apps should disable public network access
- Container Apps environment should disable public network access

These are the only policy areas currently identified for possible exemption.

Datadog reference for Azure setup / Log Forwarder:
https://docs.datadoghq.com/getting_started/integrations/azure/

Datadog reference for Azure manual log forwarding:
https://docs.datadoghq.com/logs/guide/azure-manual-log-forwarding/

Decision:
- no tenant-wide exemption
- no blanket exemption for all Datadog resources
- exemptions only if the Log Forwarder cannot be deployed in a compliant way
- exemption scope should be the Log Forwarder Resource Group or specific resources only
- exemption category: Waiver
- expiration date required
- owner required
- Jira ticket reference required
- Security/Governance approval required

Microsoft Azure Policy exemption reference:
https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/governance/policy/concepts/exemption-structure

Implementation notes:
- US-0001: create new Datadog Integration App Registration / Service Principal via tf-common application module
- US-002: extend Datadog integration only to agreed target subscriptions / management groups via tf-ecosystems / common-ecosystem module
- create dedicated Resource Group for Datadog Log Forwarder resources, name TBD
- do not remove existing PoC Service Principal role assignments yet, as some teams may still depend on it

Final position:
Initial App Registration / RBAC setup should not need Azure Policy exemptions.
Only Log Forwarder resources may need narrow, time-bound waivers for the specific policy conflicts observed in PoC.


Answer for item 2 - Point of core installation: Subscription / Resource Group

We have 3 options for Datadog core/control-plane placement:

1. New dedicated Datadog ecosystem / subscription
2. Existing Cloud Platform subscription
3. AM_S / shared resources subscription

Recommendation:
Use option 1: dedicated Datadog subscription + dedicated Resource Group for Datadog control-plane / Log Forwarder resources.

Preferred placement:
- dedicated Datadog subscription
- dedicated Resource Group, name TBD
- platform/core team ownership
- deployed via Terraform/IaC
- no shared workload resources in the same RG
- no application team ownership
- no mixing with unrelated shared infrastructure

If our internal Azure hierarchy allows it, the best fit would be under Platform / Management / Observability area.
If this does not exist in the current structure, then create a dedicated Datadog ecosystem/subscription under the agreed Landing Zones structure, but keep it platform-owned.

Why this is the preferred option:
- clean isolation of Datadog-created resources
- clear cost ownership
- easier chargeback / cost tracking
- smaller blast radius
- easier RBAC model
- easier Azure Policy exception scope if waivers are needed
- no pollution of existing Cloud Platform or shared-resource subscriptions
- better long-term operating model

Option 2 - Cloud Platform subscription:
Acceptable only as fallback or temporary placement.

Pros:
- already exists
- PoC was done there
- platform team already has access and operating model
- fastest path

Cons:
- mixes Datadog control-plane resources with existing Cloud Platform resources
- cost ownership less clean
- policy waivers could affect or complicate an existing platform subscription
- higher blast radius
- harder to separate lifecycle/offboarding later

Option 3 - AM_S / shared resources subscription:
Not recommended as target state.

Reason:
AM_S is for shared resources. Datadog Log Forwarder / control-plane resources should not be mixed with generic shared infrastructure.

Main concerns:
- unclear cost ownership
- shared blast radius
- harder RBAC separation
- harder policy exemption scope
- harder troubleshooting
- risk of mixing observability control-plane with unrelated shared services

Datadog reference:
Datadog Log Forwarder setup requires choosing:
- Resource Group name for Log Forwarder control plane
- control plane subscription
- control plane region

https://docs.datadoghq.com/getting_started/integrations/azure/

Microsoft references:
Azure subscriptions are management, billing, scale, governance, security and identity boundaries:
https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/cloud-adoption-framework/ready/landing-zone/design-area/resource-org-subscriptions

Azure Landing Zone platform area is intended for shared platform services like management, monitoring, connectivity and identity:
https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/cloud-adoption-framework/ready/landing-zone/

Management group / subscription organization guidance:
https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/cloud-adoption-framework/ready/landing-zone/design-area/resource-org-management-groups

Final position:
Target state should be a dedicated Datadog subscription and dedicated Resource Group.
Cloud Platform can be used only if we consciously accept it as temporary/fallback.
AM_S should not be used for the production Datadog core installation.


Answer for item 3 - Components needed during Datadog + Azure integration

We should split DD+AZ integration into layers. Not everything is Day-1.

Day-1 / baseline Azure integration:
- Datadog App Registration / Service Principal
- baseline Microsoft Graph permissions
- Monitoring Reader RBAC on agreed subscriptions / management groups
- Datadog Azure integration configuration
- controlled metric collection
- controlled resource collection

Log forwarding components:
- dedicated Datadog Log Forwarder Resource Group
- control-plane subscription
- control-plane region
- Storage Account used by Log Forwarder
- Datadog-created forwarding components, e.g. Function App / Container App / Managed Identity / App Service Plan / Application Insights, depending on selected deployment path
- Diagnostic Settings for selected Azure resources / subscriptions
- Log Analytics Workspace per ecosystem
- Log Analytics data export rules from LAW to the Log Forwarder Storage Account
- Defender for Cloud Continuous Export if security alerts/recommendations/secure score/compliance are in scope

Current preferred log pattern:
1. Azure resources / subscription logs / Defender data -> ecosystem Log Analytics Workspace
2. Log Analytics Workspace selected tables -> Storage Account
3. Datadog Log Forwarder -> Datadog

Important:
- do not forward everything by default
- define required log sources and tables first
- configure data export through Terraform/IaC
- onboard Defender Continuous Export to Terraform/IaC if we decide to include Defender data
- current common-ecosystem Terraform module already has parts of this pattern and should be extended/reviewed for Datadog

Agents:
- Azure VM Datadog Agent and AKS Cluster Extension should be later phase, not mandatory Day-1
- use them only where we need deeper visibility: application health, process utilization, system-level metrics, workload logs/traces, AKS details
- rollout should be scoped/piloted, not tenant-wide

